## Unit-by-unit verdicts

**Unit 1** — rejoin+split at the sentence boundary is required; the resulting sentence-2 line sits right at ~120–121 chars with two legitimate boundaries (`and`, `;`), so I treat leaving it whole and splitting at either boundary as all defensible. Anything that still ends mid-clause at "of" fails.

**Unit 2** — straightforward rejoin+split; result is well under 120, so no further splitting is warranted.

**Unit 3** — none of the candidates fully resolve the fused finding (all leave at least two of the three sentences glued together on one line); my repair isn't offered.

**Unit 4** — rejoin+split at the sentence boundary; splitting the short first sentence at the em dash is unnecessary and produces a fragment, so those variants are rejected.

**Unit 5** — none of the candidates achieve one-sentence-per-line for all five sentences (they all leave 2+ sentences fused somewhere); my repair isn't offered. Note sentence 2 (142 chars, no real clause boundary) should stay as one long line per the "leave it long" rule.

**Unit 6** — rejoin+split; result under 120, no further split needed.

**Unit 7** — rejoin+split; result short, no further split needed.

**Unit 8** — the pre-existing "because" break is a valid subordinator-led split (consistent with the checker not flagging it as wrap), so leaving it as a separate line is my primary choice, though merging it back (also correct, since it's under 120) is acceptable too.

